<!--
  Booth recovery tree for the expo rover.

  Recovery order, one action per retry (RoundRobin, 6 retries):

    1. clear costmaps            stale marks from people who already moved
    2. wait 4s                   let a person step aside
    3. back up 0.25m             give the planner room if the ROVER is the
                                 thing that is too close
    4. spin +45 deg              look left for another way through
    5. spin -90 deg              look right
    6. wait 6s                   last chance before aborting

  The order encodes which explanation is most likely, cheapest first. Two
  situations look identical to the planner and need opposite responses: a
  PERSON in the path is fixed by waiting, while the ROVER being wedged is fixed
  only by moving. Waiting is tried first because it is the common case at an
  expo and costs nothing.

  Why the spins count as "looking for another path": the behaviour tree
  re-plans after EVERY recovery. Rotating changes what the lidar can see and
  clears the robot's own orientation constraint, so if a way through exists the
  next ComputePathToPose finds it and the rover simply carries on. There is no
  separate "check" step to write.

  +45 then -90 leaves the rover 45 deg right of where it started, which is
  deliberate: RotationShim will turn it onto whatever path is found, so ending
  square with the original heading buys nothing.

  Spin was briefly removed on the reasoning that rotating beside a booth table
  unsettles visitors. That was overreach: it is the only recovery that changes
  what the sensors can see, and without it a wedged rover has no way to find an
  alternative route.

  BackUp and Spin are both collision-checked against the local costmap
  (simulate_ahead_time in behavior_server), and the lidar covers 360 degrees,
  so the rear is sensed even though the camera faces forward.

  RETURNING TO BASE when nothing works is deliberately NOT here. A behaviour
  tree recovery cannot know where "base" is, and encoding a fixed pose in this
  file would break the moment the map changed. When every recovery is exhausted
  the goal ABORTS, and the layer that sent the goal decides what that means:
  for the demo navigator, abort means tell the visitor and drive home. See
  SESSION_STATE.md.
-->

<!--
  This Behavior Tree replans the global path periodically at 1 Hz and it also has
  recovery actions specific to planning / control as well as general system issues.
  This will be continuous if a kinematically valid planner is selected.
-->
<root main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="8" name="NavigateRecovery">
      <PipelineSequence name="NavigateWithReplanning">
        <RateController hz="1.0">
          <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="ComputePathToPose">
            <ComputePathToPose goal="{goal}" path="{path}" planner_id="GridBased"/>
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Context" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
          </RecoveryNode>
        </RateController>
        <RecoveryNode number_of_retries="1" name="FollowPath">
          <FollowPath path="{path}" controller_id="FollowPath"/>
          <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Context" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
        </RecoveryNode>
      </PipelineSequence>
      <ReactiveFallback name="RecoveryFallback">
        <GoalUpdated/>
        <RoundRobin name="RecoveryActions">
          <Sequence name="ClearingActions">
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearLocalCostmap-Subtree" service_name="local_costmap/clear_entirely_local_costmap"/>
            <ClearEntireCostmap name="ClearGlobalCostmap-Subtree" service_name="global_costmap/clear_entirely_global_costmap"/>
          </Sequence>
          <Wait wait_duration="4"/>
          <BackUp backup_dist="0.25" backup_speed="0.10"/>
          <Spin spin_dist="0.79"/>
          <Spin spin_dist="-1.57"/>
          <Wait wait_duration="6"/>
        </RoundRobin>
      </ReactiveFallback>
    </RecoveryNode>
  </BehaviorTree>
</root>
